   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the racing, pari-mutuel wagering and breeding law, in relation\nto improvement of operation in thoroughbred racing",
    "basePrintNo": "S9334",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9334-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §218, RWB L",
    "lawSection": "Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9334\n\nSPONSOR: ADDABBO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the racing, pari-mutuel wagering and breeding law, in\nrelation to improvement of operation in thoroughbred racing\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThis bill would change the procedure for persons to act as temporary\nsubstitute stewards in Thoroughbred racing in the absence or inability\nto act of a steward appointed by the Gaming Commission or the failure or\ninability to appoint either of the other two stewards.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends section 218 of the Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and\nBreeding Law to allow the Gaming Commission to designate a person to\nexercise the powers and duties of Thoroughbred racing steward if the\nappointed State steward is absent or unable to act, or if there is a\nfailure or inability to appoint either of the other two stewards (one of\nwhich is appointed by the Jockey Club and the other of which is\nappointed by the racetrack).\n\nSection 2 provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrent law is carried forward from the former Racing and Wagering\nBoard, which merged into the Gaming Commission in 2013. The members of\nthe former Racing and Wagering Board had been compensated, full-time\nemployees of the State. As such, it was realistic to add to their job\nduties the necessity of filling in for a steward at a Thoroughbred race-\ntrack, if necessary. The members of the Gaming Commission, on the other\nhand, are uncompensated volunteers who have jobs outside of government.\nSee Racing, Pari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law § 102. It would be\nunduly burdensome for any Commission member to devote uncompensated time\nto performing temporary steward duties. Furthermore, a Commissioner may\nlack a background and expertise in on-track racing matters. See Racing,\nPari-Mutuel Wagering and Breeding Law § 102(2). Thoroughbred operations\nwould be improved if, in the absence of a steward, the Gaming Commission\ncould designate a person to perform such duties and have the flexibility\nto compensate such person for doing so, should the Commission deem it\nappropriate.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2016: The substantively similar S7190 passed the Senate in 2016.\n\n2017: The substantively similar S5376 passed the Senate in 2017.\n\n2018: The substantively similar S5376 passed the. Senate in 2018.\n\n2019: The substantively similar S6390 died in Senate Rules Committee.\n\n2020: The substantively similar S6390 died in Senate Racing, Gaming and\nWagering Committee.  Stylistic edits that were in S6390 were enacted in\nL.2020 c.243.\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThere would be minimal fiscal impact. There has been no need for the\ndesignation of a temporary steward since the creation of the Commission\nin 2013, but should the need arise, it would likely be appropriate for\nthe Commission to compensate such a designee for performing steward\nduties.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
